iein had always since childhood been  stronger and smarter than most of the  people around her which is great in  certain respects and very lonely in  other respects  I knew a great deal about her I'd known  her for 19 years and saw her constantly  during those years but I learned a lot  about Tiran researching the book when  you spent two-and-a-half years learning  about someone if you don't learn new  things about them you're not doing your  job properly  I spoke to over 200 people who had known  her from the time she was a child in  Russia up until the present time of the  book one of the things I learned that I  hadn't known was that her certainty  about herself had always existed  I talked to people who knew her when she  had just come from Russia and she was  talking then and when she barely spoke  English about writing a great novel and  she was totally convinced that she would  do it that she would know how to do it  people were not listened with the grain  of self to say the least  but she always knew what she wanted to  do always was determined to do it and  always was convinced that she could do  it  she was remarkable in that sense I I  never saw any any self-doubt in her any  doubt of her own powers of her own  ability so I can't say this was  something new what I learned is that she  had always been that way  not just during the years I knew her  I also learned some things I hadn't  known or at least known not fully known  that there were a lot of fears in her  life that which she didn't talk about  that she wasn't proud of the mind would  never have been proud of of anything  that frightened her but there were a lot  of fears for instance she would never  consider traveling to Europe because by  russian law she was still a russian  citizen and she was terrified that the  russians would kidnap her and take her  back to russia but they would know she  was an anti-communist she had a fear of  germs she would almost boil dishes when  she watched them that was certainly  understandable in Russia if you weren't  afraid of germs  you didn't understand the situation so I  discovered both strengths and weaknesses  in her and it was a fascinating process  she was a brilliant teacher and that's a  parent in a lot of her lectures and then  certainly in her conversations she had  an ability to know to sense some of what  her listeners needed to know in order to  grasp something and she she was a  wonderful wonderful teacher especially  the early days of knowing her when I  brought her all the questions of  adolescents that have been troubling me  is their free will things of that nature  what's moral how do we know what's good  and what's bad and it was just so  wonderful to have her start talking to  have those questions and those doubts  just fall away because of the sheer  logic of her presentation and I don't  mean I just sat and accepted at all we  would discuss argue but I felt like  she's answering questions that I've been  looking for answers for and nobody has  given me any sort of answer until now I  would bring her some of my textbooks and  I was studying philosophy and I would  read something in a philosopher that  didn't make sense to me but I couldn't  quite name what's wrong with it why why  is it not right if I was really stumped  I would bring the problem to her and the  problem would fall away in ten minutes  but that in mind that the joy of  watching that mind had worked was  something I've never experienced before  or since and I never expect to it was an  aesthetic pleasure to watch that mind at  work as well as a pleasure in the sense  of the ideas that she held and and  promulgated but it was a work of art  just to the functioning of that mind and  I never got over being thrilled by it  even sometimes when it was directed  against me I'd still be aware of what a  brilliant presentation of what's wrong  with me  well he represents in a way he  represents iron Rand because most of her  life was spent defending ability most of  John God's life was spent defending  ability which is why he organized the  strike in the first place so that in  that respect John gaunt design rant her  books were the equivalent of his strike  in that they were intended to say to the  world you had better start recognizing  human beings of ability or you're not  going to have a world left that was  God's message and that was irons message  from from we the living on it I hadn't  thought of it in these terms before you  asked the question but yes John Gogh was  iron random  my favorite section of the book that is  very strong impact on me  it's a scene Riordan is in his office  and he's thinking back on his life and  how he reached the stage success he now  enjoys and he's thinking of and he's  he's at a point where he's worn out  fighting and he slumped across his desk  feeling like he simply doesn't have the  power to he's not gonna be able to move  again he's just tired and sick and  disgusted with the impediments that have  been put in his way and he's thinking  about the stages by which he got to his  present success the nights the week's  the months of working of keeping at it  no matter what and then he thinks what  was the mode of power for my rise what  made it possible and then I earn rights  then with the greatest strength of his  life he slowly lifted himself off the  desk and sat up straight and began  working again the mode of power was his  determination and that rang all sorts of  bells for me it was very personally  meaningful and I will never forget that
 

